WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.000
Can a retina full of spikes still fail to sharpen the brain's map?

2
00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:09.750
This test reads a different map.

3
00:00:10.630 --> 00:00:12.050
Not which eye, but where in the eye.

4
00:00:13.033 --> 00:00:15.233
Label a few neighbouring ganglion cells.

5
00:00:16.133 --> 00:00:21.153
In the superior colliculus, in the midbrain, their axons end in a zone, small if the

6
00:00:21.153 --> 00:00:22.153
map is sharp.

7
00:00:22.833 --> 00:00:28.133
Todd McLaughlin, Christine Torborg, Marla Feller and Dennis O'Leary studied mice

8
00:00:28.133 --> 00:00:30.253
lacking the beta2 nicotinic subunit.

9
00:00:31.283 --> 00:00:34.863
In their recordings, these mice had no waves in the first week.

10
00:00:35.703 --> 00:00:38.623
Yet their ganglion cells fired often, without correlation.

11
00:00:39.767 --> 00:00:44.127
Predict. By day 8, are the mutants' zones small, or broad?

12
00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:55.190
Broad. Normal mice had small zones; the mutants' stayed broad.

13
00:00:56.233 --> 00:01:00.333
Later waves and vision did not fix them, which points to an early window.

14
00:01:01.333 --> 00:01:05.833
In these recordings they had plenty of spikes, but not the normal correlation

15
00:01:05.833 --> 00:01:06.833
between neighbours.

16
00:01:07.683 --> 00:01:11.463
Later labs found altered, not absent, waves in these mice.

17
00:01:11.503 --> 00:01:13.543
So the safe claim is narrower.

18
00:01:14.617 --> 00:01:19.457
Normally patterned activity, not just spiking, is needed early for the map to

19
00:01:19.457 --> 00:01:20.457
sharpen.

20
00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:22.770
Last, a detector for together.

21
00:01:23.690 --> 00:01:26.590
A frog's tectum normally gets axons from one eye.

22
00:01:27.533 --> 00:01:32.913
Martha Constantine-Paton and Margaret Law gave frog embryos an extra eye, so two

23
00:01:32.913 --> 00:01:34.353
eyes shared one tectum.

24
00:01:35.317 --> 00:01:39.577
The two projections formed alternating stripes, where none normally exist.

25
00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:46.330
Hollis Cline, Elizabeth Debski and Constantine-Paton then laid APV on the tectum.

26
00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:48.550
It blocks NMDA receptors.

27
00:01:49.733 --> 00:01:54.693
These receptors pass little current unless glutamate is bound and the membrane is

28
00:01:54.693 --> 00:01:55.893
already depolarized.

29
00:01:56.633 --> 00:01:58.453
So they can report coincident input.

30
00:01:59.433 --> 00:02:04.053
Predict. What happens to the stripes while APV is on the tectum?

31
00:02:10.417 --> 00:02:13.937
The stripes were lost, while the retinal axons still responded.

32
00:02:14.857 --> 00:02:17.297
After APV was removed, the stripes returned.

33
00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:21.250
In the frog, keeping the stripes needed these receptors.

34
00:02:22.210 --> 00:02:25.990
Which detector sorts the two eyes in the mammalian thalamus is less clear.

35
00:02:26.983 --> 00:02:28.903
Takeaway: timing is the address.

36
00:02:29.903 --> 00:02:34.563
In the models, inputs compete, and the evidence so far says that patterned firing,

37
00:02:35.003 --> 00:02:37.843
not just spike count, matters for which inputs stay.
